    },
    "transcript": "I dayd my life as a designer on learning perfectionism. I used to think perfectionism made me good at design, but really it was how I hid. I hated when people watched me work because I didn't want anyone to see the messy middle, the bad drafts, the overthinking, the doubt. I wanted to disappear until it felt perfect and polished. But when I started sharing my work before I felt ready, something changed. People didn't judge me, they added ideas, asked questions, and helped make the work better. That's when I realized perfectionism wasn't making me better, it was just keeping me invisible. Have you ever kept your work to yourself until it felt perfect?",
